## Task family: periodic diagonal color field from diagonal markers

Observed task-2b419c05. Nonzero input pixels lie on long NW-SE diagonals (constant `c-r`); use them as samples of a cyclic 1D palette indexed by the diagonal offset. Fill the entire grid with the corresponding repeating diagonal bands: `output[r][c] = palette[(c-r) mod p]`, where `p` is the period determined from the marker arrangement and demos. Palette entries are colors, not color IDs in a presumed sequence; read each marker's color at its residue, then use the full demonstrations to resolve any residues not directly marked.

Evidence from the solved 16x16 test: marker diagonals had `c-r=4 -> 6`, `5 -> 9`, `6 -> 8`, and `-9 (mod 4 = 3) -> 5`; the output used period 4 with palette residues `[6,9,8,5]`. Thus row 0 repeats `6,9,8,5`, while each lower row shifts one place left (equivalently index by `(c-r) mod 4`). The submitted grid was confirmed correct.

More evidence from demos: Demo 3 had period 4 with palette residues `0..3 = [9,5,7,8]`, and output rows were `[9,5,7,8]` repeated, shifted one place left per row. Demo 2 had period 7 palette `[2,7,3,5,1,3,9]`, likewise generated by `(c-r) mod 7`. Demo 1 is a degenerate horizontally uniform sample: the blank top row was filled with the last row's color 7 and other rows remained unchanged, consistent with cyclic continuation at a boundary, but avoid overgeneralizing this single example.

Procedure: (1) Identify the parallel diagonal families and their constant `c-r` offsets. (2) Infer the smallest demonstrated period from the repeated offset/color structure; do not assume the grid width is the period. (3) Assign each observed marker color to its offset residue; resolve missing residues by reading the demo's output palette. (4) Fill every cell according to its diagonal residue, shifting palette left by one on each successive row. Validate that all original markers are reproduced exactly. This rule is particularly reliable when marker diagonals cover enough residues to identify the palette; use another demo rather than guessing if not.
